  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Tuesday...\n\nAs a shortwave trough exits Montana today, isolated showers and a\nfew weak thunderstorms are expected to develop this afternoon. \nExpect the chance for precipitation to exist mainly over the \nmountains and foothills west of Billings. Gusty winds and brief \nheavy rainfall are the primary concerns with any storms into this \nevening. Otherwise, the rest of the forecast area will see \ndecreasing cloud cover with drier conditions and temperatures in \nthe 70s today.\n\nWarmer and drier conditions take hold Thursday and Friday, with \nhighs in the 70s to low 80sF. Friday looks to be the warmest of \nthe two days. A large surface high will influence winds over far \neastern Montana to end the week, with persistent south to \nsoutheast flow. As for south-central and southwestern Montana, \nflow will begin to shift to the southwest on Friday, ahead of a \ncold front. Windy conditions are currently forecast for the high \ncountry, with gusts in the range of 30 to 50 mph. Across the \nfoothills west of Billings gusts of 20 to 40 mph are forecast, \nwhile the rest of the area will see 15 to 30 mph gusts.\n\nThe cold front is associated with a deepening upper low that is \nset to move through Alberta this weekend. Ensembles do depict a \nshortwave moving through the region ahead of the parent trough, \nFriday afternoon. Little to impacts are expected with this wave \nthough.\n\nConfidence remains high that temperatures will drop to near \nnormals (upper 60s to low 70sF) beginning Saturday and lasting \ninto next week. Uncertainty remains high with another potential \nsystem Sunday into Monday. The latest model trends with this \nsystem show better chances for precipitation over southeastern \nMontana and northern Wyoming. Continue to monitor the forecast \ngoing into the weekend. Matos",
